WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.204 --> 00:00:01.804
AARON SIDFORD: Thank you for the introduction.

00:00:01.804 --> 00:00:02.464
Hi, everyone.

00:00:02.464 --> 00:00:03.654
I am Aaron Sidford.

00:00:03.654 --> 00:00:07.284
I am one of the many organizers
of this current program

00:00:07.284 --> 00:00:10.524
happening on data structures
and optimization for algorithm

00:00:10.524 --> 00:00:12.057
design or something like that.

00:00:12.057 --> 00:00:14.724
So I'm fortunate to be organized
in the program along with Alina

00:00:14.724 --> 00:00:17.634
Ene, Monica Henzinger,
Yin Tat Lee, and Jelani.

00:00:17.634 --> 00:00:19.434
And I guess in the
three minutes I have--

00:00:19.434 --> 00:00:20.824
I thought I had five minutes--

00:00:20.824 --> 00:00:23.154
I'm just going to give you
a very brief sense of what

00:00:23.154 --> 00:00:26.454
this program is on data
structures and optimization

00:00:26.454 --> 00:00:28.234
for fast algorithms.

00:00:28.234 --> 00:00:29.874
So there's a number
of different things

00:00:29.874 --> 00:00:33.724
that's motivating this program,
happening this quarter.

00:00:33.724 --> 00:00:36.184
One of the key motivations
underneath this program

00:00:36.184 --> 00:00:37.864
happening is a
number of advances

00:00:37.864 --> 00:00:40.834
that have happened in the last
few years in algorithm design.

00:00:40.834 --> 00:00:44.554
So for a wide range of our
fundamental, foundational,

00:00:44.554 --> 00:00:46.534
algorithmic, and
optimization problems,

00:00:46.534 --> 00:00:49.504
like convex programming,
linear system solving,

00:00:49.504 --> 00:00:52.084
analyzing [? random ?]
[? walks ?] in Markov chains,

00:00:52.084 --> 00:00:55.204
computing flows and graphs,
optimizing Markov decision

00:00:55.204 --> 00:00:58.684
processes, for this broad range
of some of our foundational

00:00:58.684 --> 00:01:00.694
algorithmic and
optimization tasks,

00:01:00.694 --> 00:01:03.364
there's been breakthrough after
breakthrough, in many cases,

00:01:03.364 --> 00:01:06.154
improving runtimes that haven't
seen improvements for decades,

00:01:06.154 --> 00:01:08.014
and in some cases,
for the first time,

00:01:08.014 --> 00:01:10.774
achieving some of these Holy
Grail results of provably

00:01:10.774 --> 00:01:14.554
solving these problems in nearly
linear or almost linear time.

00:01:14.554 --> 00:01:17.164
And what's I think particularly
exciting about these results,

00:01:17.164 --> 00:01:20.554
beyond just they've been the
pursuit of algorithm design

00:01:20.554 --> 00:01:23.944
for decades, is the wide
range of tools and techniques

00:01:23.944 --> 00:01:25.594
that have underlying
these advances.

00:01:25.594 --> 00:01:28.654
So many of these results, they
use different optimization

00:01:28.654 --> 00:01:31.654
methods like gradient descent,
interior point methods,

00:01:31.654 --> 00:01:33.064
and multiplicative weights.

00:01:33.064 --> 00:01:34.864
They use a variety
of data structures

00:01:34.864 --> 00:01:37.144
to then implement these
optimization methods

00:01:37.144 --> 00:01:38.884
efficiently, different
sorts of ways

00:01:38.884 --> 00:01:42.064
of maintaining decompositions
of large graphs, different ways

00:01:42.064 --> 00:01:44.524
of processing matrices to
get information out of them

00:01:44.524 --> 00:01:45.252
efficiently.

00:01:45.252 --> 00:01:47.044
And then in enabling
those data structures,

00:01:47.044 --> 00:01:49.054
they also use a variety
of sketching techniques,

00:01:49.054 --> 00:01:51.154
maybe some techniques we
think of for processing

00:01:51.154 --> 00:01:55.564
very large data sets or working
in low memory and in low space.

00:01:55.564 --> 00:01:57.694
And there's a number of
rich, interesting ways

00:01:57.694 --> 00:01:59.644
these tools and techniques
have been combined.

00:01:59.644 --> 00:02:02.614
So some of these
results, they work

00:02:02.614 --> 00:02:06.844
by using the optimization method
to reduce the optimization

00:02:06.844 --> 00:02:09.454
problem to a data structure
problem that is then solved

00:02:09.454 --> 00:02:11.014
using sketching techniques.

00:02:11.014 --> 00:02:13.594
Some of these works use
the optimization methods

00:02:13.594 --> 00:02:16.564
that solve these problems
dynamizing the optimization

00:02:16.564 --> 00:02:19.234
method or using the
optimization theory to reduce

00:02:19.234 --> 00:02:22.714
dynamic problems to solving
sequence of static optimization

00:02:22.714 --> 00:02:23.434
problems.

00:02:23.434 --> 00:02:25.144
And given all these
rich connections,

00:02:25.144 --> 00:02:28.234
and has been discussed, one of
the many roles of the program

00:02:28.234 --> 00:02:31.424
and tying together researchers
from a variety of areas,

00:02:31.424 --> 00:02:34.534
the goal of the program is we
have researchers and experts

00:02:34.534 --> 00:02:37.534
in all these different areas of
data structures, optimization,

00:02:37.534 --> 00:02:39.454
sketching, and we're
bringing them together

00:02:39.454 --> 00:02:41.644
towards getting
faster algorithms.

00:02:41.644 --> 00:02:43.354
The goal is to find
new connections

00:02:43.354 --> 00:02:46.264
between these areas, get new
advancements, more efficient

00:02:46.264 --> 00:02:49.804
algorithms, hopefully open the
door to more areas of research

00:02:49.804 --> 00:02:51.694
and push all the
amazing research that's

00:02:51.694 --> 00:02:53.284
already happening.

00:02:53.284 --> 00:02:56.194
We've already had three
workshops complete.

00:02:56.194 --> 00:02:57.544
We have one last one scheduled.

00:02:57.544 --> 00:02:59.044
If you're interested
in the program,

00:02:59.044 --> 00:03:00.874
you can check out the
videos from the workshops

00:03:00.874 --> 00:03:02.104
that have already happened.

00:03:02.104 --> 00:03:03.404
And I think I'm out of time.

00:03:03.404 --> 00:03:05.072
So thank you.

00:03:05.072 --> 00:03:08.425
[APPLAUSE]

